    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d455">

                <pb facs="0543" n="1297" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp02v02/pg1297"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111Sp1/50</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Spain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-11-13T12:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 13, 1917, noon</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Received November 14, 12.05 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>879. The British Ambassador called an informal meeting to-day at which were
                    present chiefs of missions of France, Italy, Russia, <pb facs="0544" n="1298"
                        xml:id="pg1298"/> United States, Japan, Belgium, Serbia, Greece, and
                    Portugal, to discuss the question of urging the Spanish Government to intern the
                    crews of German merchant vessels now in Spanish ports. Although without actual
                    proof, it was unanimously agreed that these ships are used as bases where German
                    submarines receive supplies, information, and even crews. It was further agreed
                    that all above representatives should request authorization of their Governments
                    to support the note of the French Ambassador (see Embassy’s telegram 852,
                    October 27, 10 a.m., and despatch 909, November 1<anchor n="1" xml:id="d455-f1"
                    />), suggesting the advisability of interning such merchant crews. The opinion
                    seemed to be that there was little hope that the suggestion would be accepted
                    but that it was worth while to make an attempt and put the Spanish Government on
                    record. If this internment spontaneous, I believe it would remove the chief
                    abuse of which the Department complains in its confidential telegram 746,
                    November 10, 7 p.m.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d455-f2"/> Therefore request
                    Department’s instruction to send note in the mentioned [sense]. The British
                    Ambassador has secured such authorization. In his note to Minister of State he
                    will suggest as alternative to internment of crews if that is impossible, that
                    the Spanish Government send all German vessels to one port, preferably
                    Algeciras, where they could be carefully watched and could not be used as
                    submarine bases. Personally I do not think Spanish Government will consider this
                    latter request.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
